==Broadcasting==

The MLS Cup final was televised in the United States on [[ESPN on ABC|ABC]] in English and Spanish. The network moved the start time of the match by one hour from 1:30&nbsp;p.m. [[Eastern Time Zone|Eastern Time]] to 12:30&nbsp;p.m. to accommodate scheduling changes caused by the September 11 attacks.<ref name="MLS-Oct">{{cite press release |date=October 1, 2001 |title=MLS Cup Start Time Moved To 12:30ET |url=http://www.mlsnet.com/content/01/mls1001mlscuptime.html |publisher=Major League Soccer |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20020630191413/http://www.mlsnet.com/content/01/mls1001mlscuptime.html |archive-date=June 30, 2002 |access-date=March 7, 2019}}</ref> The English commentary crew consisted of [[Jack Edwards (sportscaster)|Jack Edwards]] for play-by-play, [[Ty Keough]] with [[Color commentator|color analysis]], and other programming hosted by [[Rob Stone (sportscaster)|Rob Stone]] and [[Dave Dir]].<ref name="MLS-Oct"/> The Spanish broadcast was transmitted over [[Second audio program|secondary audio programming]] on ABC and was headlined by play-by-play commentator [[Hammer Londoño]] and color analyst [[Hernan Pereyra]] of [[Radio Unica]]. The match was also broadcast in 108 other countries by [[ESPN International]].<ref>{{cite press release |date=October 16, 2001 |title=Down To The Wire |url=http://www.mlsnet.com/content/01/weekly1016.html |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20011109070329/http://www.mlsnet.com/content/01/weekly1016.html |archive-date=November 9, 2001 |access-date=March 7, 2019}}</ref> The ABC broadcast earned a 1.0 [[Nielsen rating]], beating the previous two editions of the cup.<ref>{{cite news |last=Kennedy |first=Paul |date=December 4, 2012 |title=MLS Cup television ratings dip |url=https://www.socceramerica.com/publications/article/49487/mls-cup-television-ratings-dip.html |work=Soccer America |url-access=subscription |access-date=July 29, 2019}}</ref>

==Match==

===Summary===
[[File:DwayneDeRosario.jpg|thumb|right|upright=1.0|[[Dwayne De Rosario]] scored the [[golden goal]] to win the MLS Cup for San Jose and was named the match MVP.|alt=Dwayne De Rosario, wearing a blue jersey, prepares to strike a soccer ball on the ground with his right foot.]]

The match was played only four days after the MLS Cup semifinals, in front of a predominantly neutral crowd of 21,626 at Columbus Crew Stadium with several thousand no-show ticketholders.<ref name="Goff">{{cite news |last=Goff |first=Steven |date=October 22, 2001 |title=From Bottom to Top |page=D14 |url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A30928-2001Oct21.html |work=The Washington Post |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref> A group of about 100 Chicago Fire supporters appeared at the match to taunt the Galaxy with chants and drums.<ref>{{cite news |last=Pucin |first=Diane |date=October 22, 2001 |title=He's Just What MLS Needs |page=D11 |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2001-oct-22-sp-60186-story.html |work=Los Angeles Times |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref> The pregame ceremony included an appearance by six members of the [[New York City Fire Department]] and [[New York City Police Department]] and the national anthem sung by [[Toya (singer)|Toya]].<ref name="Notes">{{cite press release |date=October 18, 2001 |title=MLS Cup 2001 News & Notes |url=https://www.oursportscentral.com/services/releases/mls-cup-2001-news--notes/n-2708532 |publisher=Major League Soccer |via=Our Sports Central |access-date=March 7, 2019}}</ref><ref name="BH">{{cite news |last=Martins |first=Gus |date=October 22, 2001 |title=Schmid, Galaxy must kick back |page=72 |work=[[The Boston Herald]]}}</ref> The match was the first MLS Cup to be refereed by [[Kevin Stott]], who previously served as the fourth official at [[MLS Cup 2000]].<ref name="Notes"/><ref>{{cite web |title=MLS Cup Roll of Honor |url=http://proreferees.com/officials/mls-cup-roll-of-honor/ |publisher=[[Professional Referee Organization]] |access-date=March 7, 2019}}</ref>

The Earthquakes began the match with the majority of possession, which was used to build up to several attacking chances that were cleared away by Galaxy goalkeeper [[Kevin Hartman]]. Los Angeles, however, had a chance of its own in the 14th minute on a cross by [[Cobi Jones]] towards [[Sasha Victorine]] that was passed on to [[Mauricio Cienfuegos]], whose shot was cleared away by San Jose. The Galaxy opened the scoring in 21st minute on a goal by [[Luis Hernández (footballer, born 1968)|Luis Hernández]], who received a {{convert|40|yd|m|adj=mid}} pass from defender [[Greg Vanney]].<ref name="SI">{{cite news |date=October 21, 2001 |title=Worst to first: Golden goal lifts Quakes over Galaxy 2-1 in MLS Cup |url=http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/mls/2001/mls_cup/news/2001/10/21/mlscup_gamer/ |work=Sports Illustrated |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20121113001048/http://sportsillustrated.cnn.com/soccer/mls/2001/mls_cup/news/2001/10/21/mlscup_gamer/ |archive-date=November 13, 2012 |access-date=March 4, 2019}}</ref> Los Angeles then missed an opportunity to take a 2–0 lead in the 35th minute after a shot from Cobi Jones was saved by goalkeeper [[Joe Cannon (soccer)|Joe Cannon]].<ref name="LAT-Report">{{cite news |date=October 22, 2001 |title=MLS Cup Game Report |page=D11 |work=Los Angeles Times |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/28281436/mls_cup_game_report/ |via=Newspapers.com |access-date=March 6, 2019}}</ref> The Earthquakes found an equalizing goal shortly before half-time in the 43rd minute on a play started by [[Ian Russell (soccer)|Ian Russell]]'s run down the right wing. He slipped a pass to [[Richard Mulrooney]], whose cross over the box was volleyed by [[Landon Donovan]] into the top-right corner of the goal.<ref name="Goff"/><ref name="SI"/>

San Jose went on to outshoot Los Angeles 12–3 in the second half and created several chances to score, including a free kick taken by [[Jeff Agoos]] that hit the left goalpost.<ref name="Goff"/><ref name="SI"/> After receiving a [[yellow card (sport)|yellow card]] for a challenge on Ian Russell, Galaxy defender [[Paul Caligiuri]] was substituted in the 53rd minute—marking the end of his MLS career.<ref name="LAT-Report"/> Three additional yellow cards were issued in the second half to [[Danny Califf]], [[Ronnie Ekelund]], and [[Zak Ibsen]]; Ibsen had only been substituted a minute before drawing the card.<ref name="BH"/><ref name="LAT-Report"/> [[Dwayne De Rosario]] was substituted into the game in the 85th minute for [[Ronald Cerritos]], shortly before the tied match forced a sudden-death overtime.<ref name="SI"/> De Rosario scored the winning [[golden goal]] in the sixth minute of overtime with a dribble around Califf and a shot from {{convert|17|yd|m}} that Hartman managed to touch, but was unable to save.<ref>{{cite news |last=Payne |first=Dave |date=October 22, 2001 |title=DeRosario's overtime goal lifts Quakes to MLS crown |url=http://www.bayarea.com/c/category/sports_and_recreation/soccer/san_jose_earthquakes/docs/mercurynews/quakes22x.htm |work=The Mercury News |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20011211224401/http://www.bayarea.com/c/category/sports_and_recreation/soccer/san_jose_earthquakes/docs/mercurynews/quakes22x.htm |archive-date=December 11, 2001 |access-date=March 3, 2019}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |last=Chapin |first=Dwight |date=October 22, 2001 |title=Earthquakes' victory is extra special |url=https://www.sfgate.com/sports/article/Earthquakes-victory-is-extra-special-2866446.php |work=San Francisco Chronicle |access-date=March 7, 2019}}</ref> It was the second time in MLS Cup history that the title was decided by a golden goal, following the [[MLS Cup 1996|inaugural edition]] between D.C. United and the Galaxy.<ref>{{cite news |date=October 21, 2001 |title=DeRosario Stunner Gives San Jose the Title |url=http://www.mlsnet.com/games/01/mlscup2001.html |work=MLSnet.com |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20011122133507/http://www.mlsnet.com/games/01/mlscup2001.html |archive-date=November 22, 2001 |access-date=March 3, 2019}}</ref>

