==Post-match==

The Earthquakes completed a "worst to first" finish, which was named the greatest turnaround in MLS history.<ref name="AP-Summary">{{cite news |last=Gillespie |first=Charley |date=October 22, 2001 |title=Earthquakes Shake L.A. To Capture MLS Cup |page=B5 |work=[[Albuquerque Journal]] |agency=Associated Press |url=https://www.newspapers.com/clip/28281322/earthquakes_shake_la_to_capture_mls/ |via=Newspapers.com |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref> Frank Yallop became the first former MLS player to win the MLS Cup as head coach, as well as the first non-American manager to win the championship.<ref name="Goff"/><ref>{{cite news |last=Wise |first=Mike |date=November 15, 2004 |title=United on Top of Its Game |page=A1 |url=https://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A50056-2004Nov14.html |work=The Washington Post |access-date=March 17, 2019}}</ref> Dwayne De Rosario was named the MLS Cup MVP for his golden goal, despite entering the match in the 85th minute.<ref>{{cite news |last=Robledo |first=Fred J. |date=October 22, 2001 |title=This one's just not in the stars for Galaxy |page=B1 |work=Long Beach Press-Telegram}}</ref> The Galaxy lost their third final in six years, with the match against San Jose described as the "most difficult" by coach Sigi Schmid.<ref>{{cite news |last=Znidar |first=Mark |date=October 22, 2001 |title=Galaxy endures yet another cup spill |page=D3 |work=The Columbus Dispatch}}</ref> The Galaxy went on to win the [[2001 U.S. Open Cup]] a week later, defeating the [[New England Revolution]] 2–1 in overtime.<ref>{{cite news |last=Jones |first=Grahame L. |date=October 28, 2001 |title=Redemption Corner for Califf, Galaxy |page=D3 |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2001-oct-28-sp-62639-story.html |work=Los Angeles Times |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref> Los Angeles met New England again at [[MLS Cup 2002]], winning 1–0 in overtime for their first MLS Cup title.<ref>{{cite news |last=Goff |first=Steven |date=October 21, 2002 |title=Galaxy Sitting on Top of World |page=D6 |url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/articles/A56525-2002Oct20.html |work=The Washington Post |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref> San Jose won their second MLS Cup in the [[MLS Cup 2003|2003 final]] at the Galaxy's newly-constructed [[soccer-specific stadium]], the [[Home Depot Center]] in [[Carson, California]], after eliminating the hosts in the playoffs.<ref>{{cite news |last=Jones |first=Grahame L. |date=November 24, 2003 |title=Earthquakes Win MLS Cup |page=D1 |url=https://www.latimes.com/archives/la-xpm-2003-nov-24-sp-mls24-story.html |work=Los Angeles Times |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref>

San Jose qualified for the [[2002 CONCACAF Champions' Cup]] alongside the finalists from [[MLS Cup 2000]], Chicago and Kansas City, due to the cancellation of the previous Champions' Cup and the transition to the new, expanded cup format.<ref>{{cite press release |date=October 10, 2001 |title=Club Competitions in the Football Confederation Reborn as FC Champions' Cup Begins New League Format for 2002 |url=https://www.ussoccer.com/stories/2014/03/17/13/58/club-competitions-in-the-football-confederation-reborn-as-fc-champons-cup-begins-new-league-format-f |publisher=[[United States Soccer Federation]] |url-status=dead |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181109115202/https://www.ussoccer.com/stories/2014/03/17/13/58/club-competitions-in-the-football-confederation-reborn-as-fc-champons-cup-begins-new-league-format-f |archive-date=November 9, 2018 |access-date=February 10, 2019 }}</ref> The Earthquakes advanced from the first round against [[Club Deportivo Olimpia|C.D. Olimpia]] by winning 1–0 at the [[Miami Orange Bowl]] and 3–1 at a small stadium in [[Sacramento, California|Sacramento]].<ref>{{cite news |last=Voet |first=Gary |date=March 17, 2002 |title=Sellout crowd sees Quakes roll |page=C2 |work=[[Sacramento Bee]]}}</ref> San Jose then faced [[C.F. Pachuca]] in the quarterfinals and lost 3–0 in the away leg played in Mexico.<ref>{{cite news |last=Chapin |first=Dwight |date=April 24, 2002 |title=Pachuca has big edge in series |page=C3 |url=https://www.sfgate.com/sports/article/Pachuca-has-big-edge-in-series-2846379.php |work=San Francisco Chronicle |access-date=February 10, 2019}}</ref> The Earthquakes rallied to win 1–0 the following week in the home leg, but were eliminated from the cup with an [[aggregate score]] of 1–3.<ref>{{cite news |last=Harper |first=Jelani |date=April 25, 2002 |title=Quakes win game, lose series |work=[[The Argus (Fremont)|The Argus]] |location=Fremont, California}}</ref>

The Earthquakes and its players were later relocated to Houston after the 2005 season, becoming the [[Houston Dynamo]]. The Dynamo won consecutive MLS Cup championships in their first two seasons after relocation, while the Earthquakes returned to the league as an [[expansion team]] in the 2008 season.<ref>{{cite news |last=Goff |first=Steven |date=November 19, 2007 |title=The Dynamo's Double Standard |page=E1 |url=http://www.washingtonpost.com/wp-dyn/content/article/2007/11/18/AR2007111800390.html |work=The Washington Post |access-date=February 14, 2019}}</ref> The Galaxy went on to win five MLS Cup titles, including two against the Dynamo in the [[MLS Cup 2011|2011]] and [[MLS Cup 2012|2012]] finals.<ref>{{cite news |last=Powers |first=John |date=December 8, 2014 |title=Galaxy defeat Revolution for record fifth MLS title |url=https://www.bostonglobe.com/sports/2014/12/07/galaxy-defeat-revolution-for-record-fifth-mls-title/QXMrUO8NT5sTEKPW5Un1UL/story.html |work=The Boston Globe |access-date=February 14, 2019}}</ref>

